+### TASK-059: the reality check of the runners' folders (2026-11-14, before the brief)
+The plan asks, before TASK-059's brief, which environment names move the folder npm, pnpm and yarn run a project's test script in, from each tool's documentation and a probe in a scratch folder (R-078; DESIGN.md, F5). The working notes are run/supervisor/TASK-059-reality-check-research.md.
+- **How it was read.** npm 11.16.0 is installed and was probed live: two scratch projects each print their own name from `scripts.test`, npm is started in one with each candidate set, and the output says which ran. pnpm and yarn are not installed, and installing a tool needs the owner's word, so neither was installed: their source at a named tag and their documentation stand in for the probe. A subagent read pnpm v9.15.9 and v10.34.9 (with `@pnpm/npm-conf`), Yarn 1.22.22, Yarn Berry `@yarnpkg/cli` 4.18.1 and npm v11.21.2, nothing run; GitHub's code search covers default branches only, so a name absent there is not proven absent from older tags. This deviation from the plan's words (a probe for each tool) is recorded here and told in the increment's report.
+- **npm, probed.** Only the command line moves the folder: `--prefix` before or after `test`, its `=` form, and `-C`, which is npm's short form of `--prefix` (`test -C <b>`, `-C <b> test`, `-C=<b>` each ran in b). An unambiguous abbreviation moves it too: `--prefi=<b>` ran in b, `--pref <b>` did not (npm reads `--pref` as ambiguous). The attached `-C<b>` and `--PREFIX <b>` ran in the launch folder; after a bare `--` the option is the script's own argument. Each of these ran in the launch folder: `npm_config_prefix`, `NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX`, `npm_config_local_prefix`, `npm_config_localprefix`, `INIT_CWD`, `PWD`, `npm_config_userconfig` and `npm_config_globalconfig` naming a file with `prefix=`, and `npm_config_global` with a prefix; npm's source agrees (`loadLocalPrefix` takes `prefix` from the command-line layer only). Two names move it: `npm_config_workspace` (a declared workspace by folder or by package name ran there, an undeclared folder exited 1) and `npm_config_workspaces=true` (every declared workspace). npm matches its variables without regard to case.
+- **pnpm, read.** No environment name sets `dir`: the folder is `--dir`/`-C` or else the local prefix found from the working folder, and `npm_config_dir` is overwritten. `npm_config_workspace_dir` (read as `NPM_CONFIG_WORKSPACE_DIR` or its lower-case form) names the workspace root, used by `-w`, `-r` and `--filter`; a plain `pnpm test` is unaffected. Not settled: an environment-level `recursive`, and `-g` with `PNPM_HOME`.
+- **Yarn, read.** Neither Yarn 1 nor Berry reads a name that sets `--cwd`. A Yarn 1 `.yarnrc` can carry `--cwd`, and `YARN_CONFIG` names such a file; Berry's `YARN_RC_FILENAME` names its configuration file, which cannot set the folder.
+- **What the rule reads, from this.** The folder options are npm's `--prefix` and `-C`, pnpm's `--dir` and `-C`, yarn's `--cwd`, wherever they stand before a bare `--`, in the two-word and the `=` form, a long option word that is a leading part of the folder option's name (three characters or more with its dashes) read as that option, the attached `-C<folder>` refused unread. The names held to the target rule are `npm_config_workspace` and `npm_config_workspaces` for npm and `npm_config_workspace_dir` for pnpm, any letter case, in the run's environment or a key file's line; none for yarn. A configuration file a runner finds stays outside the rule (an `.npmrc`'s `prefix=` or `workspace=`, a file `npm_config_userconfig` or `npm_config_globalconfig` names, pnpm's configuration folder, a `.yarnrc` and the file `YARN_CONFIG` names, a `package.json`'s declared workspaces), as INVENTORY's row for the target rule will say in the increment's records. npm's `-C` and the abbreviated forms are not in the plan's words; they are what the probe found the plan's option to be, and the brief carries them.
+- **Corrected after round 1's reviews (2026-11-14).** The reading above was incomplete. npm's parser (nopt) strips every leading dash before it matches, expands a single-dash cluster of one-letter options, and takes an unambiguous abbreviation of any length, so `-prefix`, `---prefix`, `-prefi=<b>`, `--C`, `-sC <b>`, `-dC <b>` and `-sC=<b>` each moved npm's folder. Round 1's security audit and its Review A each reproduced this with the installed npm 11, from a scratch folder; round 1 accepted every one of them. The audit also found `--script-shell <program>` (and `npm_config_script_shell`) running another program as the script shell, and `--node-options` beside it. So the rule changed from reading npm's grammar to a list, as `uv_run_pytest` holds uv's: before a bare `--`, a named npm, pnpm or yarn suite takes no word that starts with `-` but its folder options in their exact two-word and `=` forms and three harmless flags (`--silent`, `-s`, `--if-present`); every other is refused (an abbreviation, a cluster, an attached `-C<folder>`, `-w`, `--workspace`, `--script-shell`, `--node-options`); a folder value that starts with `-` is refused. For an npm or pnpm run, `npm_config_script_shell` and `npm_config_node_options` are refused when set at all, the environment's form of the options that run another program, as `PYTEST_PLUGINS` is for pytest. This list tightens the plan's words, which name the folder options only; it holds the target rule and `test_suite`'s standing promise (never an option that runs another program), and it refuses no form a loop's suite needs.
+- **Round 1's security audit, a hole older than this increment:** on Windows a `.cmd` runner is started as `cmd.exe /d /c <runner> <arguments>`, and `subprocess.list2cmdline` does not escape for cmd.exe, so `&`, `|`, `<`, `>`, `^`, `%`, `!`, a `"` that unbalances the quotes, a parenthesis or a line break in an argument reaches cmd's own parser: `npm test "&" echo X` ran the echo at 3a37bde already. Untrusted input reaches it (an agent's command line under the allow rule), so it is fixed in this increment's round 2: such an argument of a `.cmd` or `.bat` runner is refused before anything starts.
+- **Corrected after round 2's reviews (2026-11-14).** Round 2's list still let a flag through in its `=` form: npm splits a word at `=` and reads the value of a flag that takes none as a word of its own, so `npm test --silent=--prefix <out>`, `-s=--prefix=<out>` and `--if-present=--prefix=<out>` ran npm outside the launch roots (Review A and the security audit each reproduced it with npm 11.16.0, through the launcher, after `bridge-run: started`); a flag is now its exact word only, and only after the subcommand. The audit also ran an outside `npm-cli.js` by `npm_config_prefix` alone, from the environment and from a key file's line: npm.cmd's `npm-prefix.js` reads that name before npm itself starts and runs `<prefix>\node_modules\npm\bin\npm-cli.js` where it exists. The reality check above measured only the folder a test script ran in, not which npm ran, and so recorded `npm_config_prefix` as moving nothing. This is the second round on the environment rule, so its design changed instead of a third name being added: an environment name is a command-line option in another form (`npm_config_<x>` is `--<x>` to npm and pnpm, and `npm_config_userconfig` or `npm_config_globalconfig` names a file that carries every other option), so the list holds there too. For a named npm, pnpm or yarn suite, a name that begins with `npm_config_`, `pnpm_config_` (pnpm) or `yarn_` (yarn), any letter case, in the run's environment or a key file's line, is refused whatever its value, except the two cache names the bridge passes to its own runs (`NPM_CONFIG_CACHE`, `YARN_CACHE_FOLDER`). It replaces round 2's folder rule for the workspace names and its two-name refusal, and tightens the plan's words a second time (the plan holds the names found to move the folder to the target rule; this refuses every name the runner reads as an option), told to the owner as a notice (rule 58). What stays outside, in INVENTORY's row: the interpreter's own names (`NODE_OPTIONS`, as `PYTHONPATH` for python), `Co